This means that your module has an extern symbol that isn't in
the kernel. That is, you have something like:

extern int foobar;

where there is no "foobar" in the kernel.

Unfortunately, due to a shortcoming in the infrastructure, it can
also mean exactly the opposite, that there is a *duplicate* symbol
name somewhere. That is:

It would be helpful if the manager differentiated these two, and it
would be even MORE helpful if the manager specified which symbol was
undefined/redefined.

>2. #cp mymodule /sys/BINARY/
>   #cp mymodule /subsys/
>    (I didn't modify /etc/sysconfigtab file to put attributes of my
>module)
>    # sysconfig -c mymodule
>   method failed to perform preconfiguration
>   subsystem error : Not owner

Is it "mymodule" or "mymodule.mod"? I think you have to have the
.mod suffix on the file.  But, the not owner message implies that
you are not root, so don't have permission to do this.

