In article <5lels3uq9y.fsf@rum.cs.yale.edu>,
Stefan Monnier  <foo@acm.com> wrote:
>>>>>> "Nick" == Nick Maclaren <nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk> writes:
>> For example, assume one is running in kernel mode and has disabled
>> all interrupts and the second is running in user mode and needs to
>> take an interrupt.  Without GREAT care, something then goes wrong.
>
>Huh?  How is that different from the SMP case ?
>If your kernel-only-no-interrupts threads can't deal with the other
>thread being interrupted, then it should suspend that thread at the
>same time that it turns off interrupts.
>I don't see the difficulty from a comp.arch POV.

The problem is that some interrupts apply to a well-defined thread
(and hence are no trouble) and others are more global.  Consider
clock or I/O interrupts, for example.  Or consider the following
scenario:

    Thread A is handling a TLB miss or in-memory page fault, and
will be uninterruptible for the best part of a microsecond.

    A high-priority fault comes in (say clock, I/O or machine check),
so thread B takes it.

    The kernel code then needs to lock all threads while it handles
the interrupt.

Does it wait until thread A has finished, abort thread A, or does
it interrupt the uninterruptible thread?  In all cases, there are
things that can go horribly wrong.

We see this increasingly often at the software level in modern
systems, where an architecturally single-threaded design of operating
system has some threading bolted on and runs on top of a parallel
hardware architecture.  Stuck states, inconsistent results, and
heaven alone knows what else.  A bane of my life :-(


