You pushed a hot button here...

I don't speak for IBM, and I'm not working on SMT anymore.

There is indeed a disgusting trend in lowering expectations to the point
where people are willing to buy stuff that doesn't work.  I'm trying very
deliberately to distance myself from that.  I think large segments of the
company I work for do too.  

I can't imagine any responsible company following the direction you suggest,
however.  My personal approach to design is that interfaces do exactly what
they claim they do -- or report that they can't do it.  With regard to SMT,
I would expect that a design that can't handle interrupts in one thread when
another is disabled would stop the alternate thread instead of crossing those
proverbial thumbs and hoping nothing goes wrong.

Compiler hacking around hardware deficiencies may happen, but I think only
during development of new hardware (early chips with bugs that need to be
worked around -- I seem to recall a case where one of the registers could
not be used, for example), not with shipped products.    

With regard to your last sentence:  it *is* the user's fault not to put
pressure on manufacturers to produce reliable products.  Reliability costs
more, especially if unreliability sells so well.  If everything was expected
to be reliable, it wouldn't cost so much.  So as long as economics favour
selling unreliable stuff, that's what we'll get.  RESIST THE TREND!

Michel.





