Unfortunately, I am afraid that almost all do, though (a) I do not
think that it is planned and (b) it is software rather than hardware
that is the cause.  I have reasonable evidence that this class of
failures exists on all major systems I have used, though I have not
yet had enough experience on IBM POWER systems to say that for sure
in that case.

>Compiler hacking around hardware deficiencies may happen, but I think only
>during development of new hardware (early chips with bugs that need to be
>worked around -- I seem to recall a case where one of the registers could
>not be used, for example), not with shipped products.    

God, I wish :-(  I have got definite proof on a couple of systems,
and pretty convincing evidence on half a dozen others, but have
managed to get only one of the cases fixed in recent years.  I have
tried to get this fixed in the C standard for 12 years, and have
JUST got an admission from both C and POSIX that it is conforming
behaviour!

The problem is that it has the following characteristics:

    1) It occurs only in some programs compiled with aggressive
optimisation, and then often with probabilities of 1% down to
0.0001%.  Most people just rerun, and worry only if the failure rate
gets above 10%.

    2) A decreasing number of people are capable of even recognising
the symptoms correctly, let alone tracking them down.  I estimate
that the average age of such people is increasing by 5 years for
every decade that passes.

    3) Typically it shows up only in cases that are technically
undefined behaviour, which is where my standards hobby-horse comes
in.  Have you ever tried to get a vendor to fix a bug in a case
which is not specified to work?

>With regard to your last sentence:  it *is* the user's fault not to put
>pressure on manufacturers to produce reliable products.  Reliability costs
>more, especially if unreliability sells so well.  If everything was expected
>to be reliable, it wouldn't cost so much.  So as long as economics favour
>selling unreliable stuff, that's what we'll get.  RESIST THE TREND!

