That's funny, I don't find that easy at all to think of any
development-related decisions an engineer could have been in a position to
have made that would have had a noticeably negative effect on Alpha's market
share.  Lack-luster funding for the hardware cornerstone (or later, under
Compaq, at least *one* hardware cornerstone) of the corporation?  It would
be nice if engineers got to determine their own funding levels, but in my
experience it's usually someone else who's given that authority.  Luke-warm
marketing efforts?  Nope, that's not an engineering decision.  Lack of
anything like sufficient corporate support for the operating systems that
ran on Alpha to make customers feel confident of their future (i.e.,
corporate commitment to the platform)?  Nope, not really an engineering
decision either.  Churning the engineering development pool with repeated
corporate layoffs and lengthening development schedules as a result?  No, I
don't think engineers normally control that.  Really screwing up Alpha's
performance lead in the marketplace?  Well, engineers *could* have done
that, but don't seem to have, even in the face of considerable adversity
(though it has clearly been pretty tough to retain even a marginal lead
under the circumstances).

Perhaps if you provided examples of such decisions it would help.

