My understand of the 683xx is that there are _some_ 68020+ features
in the instruction set, so it's not a straight 68000. Ergo, there must
have been _some_ architecture work in deciding what to take and what to
leave. Granted, I've not done any serious work with the 683xx, so this
is just an impression taken from the manuals. And I'm not a CPU architect,
so I'm fuzzy on what precisely CPU architecture work would be. 

Let me try again:

>>  - VAX (11/780 -> NV5 sure _seemed_ like more than 12 years)

The most recent change I'm aware of to VAX is support for 35-bit physical
addresses. I don't know precisely when it happened, but I'm pretty sure
it was more than 12 years after 11/780.

>>  - MC680x0 (Macintosh -> Palm sure seemed like more than 12 years)

It is my understanding that 683xx is _not_ merely a 68000 repackaged. I
may be wrong. Someone else has mentioned ColdFire; IIRC, the original
ColdFire chips were 68060's with a list of instructions that you shouldn't
use becuase they were going away.

>>  - PDP-8 (PDP-5 in 1963 -> DECmate III until about 1993)

The 6120 added (at least) a stack-based call mechanism. I don't know precisely
when, but I'm pretty sure it was more than 12 years after the PDP-5 provided
the initial implementation of the instruction set.

>>  - PDP-11 (Call it 1970, still being built)

I don't know precisely when the J-11 happened; perhaps this isn't a good
example. Looking over Mentec's web page, it looks like they're emulating
the J-11, so they probably haven't done any architectural work.

>>  - 8080 (Early '70s, lives on in the Z80, still being extended)

Solid ground here. eZ80 is still in development, Rabbit just recently
released their processor.

>>  - 8051 (I don't know when it started, but it's been longer than 12 years)

Quoting Dallas Semiconductor's web page: "The DS89C420 features two data
pointers that can be used to move blocks of data both forwards and backwards.
This unique capability to automatically increment or decrement the data pointer
reduces the number of instructions steps and increases the speed of
operations." There's some architecture work going on there.

>>  - 8048, for that matter (runs the keyboard I'm typing on)

I'll have to give you this one; I really haven't been paying a whole lot of
attention to the 8048 guys.

>>  - x86, of course (much, much too long)

'Nuff said.

>>  - 360 -> 370 -> 390

And now to zMumble. Sure looks like architecture work to me.

>>  - Burroughs 5500 -> Unisys A-series

You can't hardly talk about stack machines without someone bringing up new
stuff happening to the A series. Granted, I don't know how much of this is
architecture work and how much is impementation work.

How'd I do?
