"Joe Pfeiffer" <pfeiffer@cs.nmsu.edu> wrote in message
news:1br8w41re8.fsf@cs.nmsu.edu...

...

> In the first Alpha paper I read, in the DEC Technical Journal, Alpha's
> lifespan was estimated at 25 years (based in part on the history of
> the VAX, which went nearly that long), and that DEC anticipated a
> 1000-fold performance improvement over that lifespan.  So I'm curious
> as to what went wrong -- what didn't pan out?  (if you can tell us, of
> course)

It wasn't the architectural vision that didn't pan out (though now we'll
never know if it *would* have panned out):  it was the commitment of the
owner.

The problems (at least those related to Alpha's present demise) started in
the mid-'90s, when DEC made a concerted effort to kill off VMS and get users
to 'migrate' (very, very touchy word in VMS circles) to NT, via the infamous
'Affinity' (another very, very touchy word) program.  In the process, the
sales force made sure to tell any potential new customers that VMS was a
dead end and NT was the future.  Don't ask why:  I've never heard a sensible
answer, save for the conspiracy theory that Palmer was hired to run DEC's
value down far enough that someone would buy it, which never made sense to
be but then I'm no economist (there's also the theory that the Unix
contingent was trying to use NT to kill VMS, which I suppose could have been
the case).

Funny thing:  the VMS market kind of flattened out right about then, since
the only users not scared off by such treatment by VMS's owner were those
that really, really needed what VMS had to offer (hence they're kind of
fanatical about it and also willing to put up with near-infinite amounts of
abuse from DECpaq).  Strike 1 against Alpha:  if you don't have a thriving
market, you can't be considered indispensible.

My impression is that the residue of the Ultrix -> OSF/1 -> DEC Unix ->
Tru64 and VAX -> MIPS -> Alpha transitions may have dampened enthusiasm for
Unix on Alpha until relatively recently (until last Monday, things seemed to
be looking up a bit, though).  Strike 2 (again, no thriving market to make
Alpha indispensible).

And when Compaq took over, the hopes VMS people had had that VMS would
finally get some marketing (Compaq being thought to be far more competent at
such than DEC ever had been, much less during the Palmer extermination
effort) turned out to be groundless:  no marketing materialized, and it also
took a while for the sales staff to stop trying to get VMS customers to
switch to just about *anything* else (well, perhaps not Solaris, though
that's where a lot of customers went after getting completely disgusted with
Compaq's migration efforts).  Again, Unix on Alpha fared a little better,
but since it didn't have the 20-year customer base to draw on, still not
that impressively (and it never got anywhere near the marketing attention of
the 'Classic Compaq' products).

So Compaq had no balls, and the count stood at 0-and-2.  An 0-and-2 product
doesn't get high funding priority, so Alpha development schedules slipped -
a lot (Terry Shannon was fond of pointing out that IA32 clock rates advanced
by something like 4 times the percentage amount Alpha clock rates advanced
during a particular lengthy period - can't remember the exact numbers,
though).  This let Alpha's initial performance lead slip away to the point
where it's now marginal rather than commanding, which prepared it for
Monday's Strike 3.

At least that's what I've heard, from people who should know:  a series of
mis-steps leading to decreased funding (other microprocessors are said to
have had an order of magnitude more, but I wouldn't presume to vouch for
that figure), rather than any problem with the initial vision or the
subsequent implementation.  Other people are welcome to chime in and offer
additions and even corrections:  since most sources fall well short of being
authoritative, at the end of it all we still have to decide for ourselves
whom to believe.

