I guess the large number of different and very complex addressing modes is a problem too.
On 68020+ data could be loaded/stored by first looking up the address of
the address in memory, including scaling factors and offsets.
This might be viewed as an advantage for assembler programmers in
times where memory was expensive and even simple integer operations
took many cycles. It's quite the opposite of the RISC principle which 
rules these days and allows high clock speeds. 


