In article <5lithfuxa4.fsf@rum.cs.yale.edu>,
"Stefan Monnier <foo@acm.com>" <monnier+comp.arch/news/@flint.cs.yale.edu> writes:
|> >>>>> "Nick" == Nick Maclaren <nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk> writes:
|> > It gets seriously tricky if you allow interrupts during the
|> > process, which is needed for really massive virtual memory
|> > systems.  Pageable page tables are not easy to support in
|> > conjunction with multi-threading.
|> 
|> I thought asynchronous interrupts would just schedule a new thread
|> (or rather wake up a special thread) while synchronous interrupts
|> (like TLB faults) would stop the offending thread (put it to sleep)
|> and start another one (maybe the very same special thread used for
|> async interrupts).

The latter is a GHASTLY idea, though POSIX came close to requiring
it.  Inter alia, scheduling synchronous interrupts in a single
thread means that things like underflow or TLB misses become even
more horribly inefficient than they need be.

And a single dedicated thread for asynchronous interrupts means that
you can handle only one level of them.  Not good news in many
environments, though less of a problem than the former one.

|> At least that seems like the most straightforward way to deal
|> with interrupts within an SMT system.  What are the problems with such an
|> approach (apart from the obvious changes in the OS) ?

It doesn't help - that's the problem.  The problems that I am
referring to come whenever you have two threads running in
different modes - HOWEVER you get there.

For example, assume one is running in kernel mode and has disabled
all interrupts and the second is running in user mode and needs to
take an interrupt.  Without GREAT care, something then goes wrong.


