Firstly, I'm an FP bigot. Also try convincing people IA-64 has
an lock alpha's integer performance nether regions and you will
be laughed at given the factor of two to EV6 in SPEC-2000.

One asks what's the killer feature IA-64 has got that Alpha
hasn't, that make it more viable beyond EV7: Registers.

Another writer commented make the pipelines longer: Keeping
a wider/longer set of issue slots full is *hard* without
enought independent regs to express the independence of the ops.

Rotating regs etc.. are just the icing that makes the cake sweeter.

However, I would have thought that Alpha adding twin fmacs (like
power) would add a generation of useful single thread performance.
(yeah yeah adds a read port, but at least fmac as opposed fmadd doesnt
need 4 operands):

The fmac eases register pressure greatly because
you don't have to blow somthing like twice the pipeline
latency in regs to software pipeline multiply add chains.

