In article <3B3B4580.25C255DD@zk3.dec.com>, Tony Verhulst <verhulst@zk3.dec.com> writes:
> Look at the history of CPU architectures VAX, MC680x0, and others.  They
> tend not to last more than 10 to 12 years.

You mean others like:

  - VAX (11/780 -> NV5 sure _seemed_ like more than 12 years)
  - MC680x0 (Macintosh -> Palm sure seemed like more than 12 years)
  - PDP-8 (PDP-5 in 1963 -> DECmate III until about 1993)
  - PDP-11 (Call it 1970, still being built)
  - 8080 (Early '70s, lives on in the Z80, still being extended)
  - 8051 (I don't know when it started, but it's been longer than 12 years)
  - 8048, for that matter (runs the keyboard I'm typing on)
  - x86, of course (much, much too long)
  - 360 -> 370 -> 390
  - Burroughs 5500 -> Unisys A-series

In fact, the vast majority of machines I use run architectures which are
much older than 12 years and are still in active development. I'm
not sure I can think of one which didn't last more than 12 years; even the
6800 lives on in the 68HC08! All those PICs everyone seems to be so enamored
with these days were started in the '70s by GI. How long did the Transputer 
last? How long did Nova->Eclipse->MV take? SC/MP; now, there's one that
wasn't around very long. Fairchild PACE? Clipper?
