Okay, consider this:  HP announced the death of PA-RISC back when IA-64 was
announced.  They've been extremely coy about their upgrade plans, always
claiming some sort of "in box" upgrade capability, but in reality likely
requiring you to swap most of the internals out and keep the shell...

Given all that, HP's PA-RISC marketshare hasn't decreased all that much
faster than what it was trending towards prior to the announcement.  The
shop I'm consulting at now just keeps on buying boxes.  Why?  Because they
buy for the OS, not necessarily the hardware/chip...

I would argue that the people that buy for the OS and it's capabilities and
functionality should perhaps consider not-as-aggressive upgrades, but not
really slow down all that much.  2004-2005 transition period is a good 3
years out yet, and most shops I've worked at look at a 3 year life span on
their hardware now anyway, except for the big iron.  And overall support
isn't disappearing tomorrow, an EOL hasn't even been announced.  In short,
if the OS does what you need and it's what your comfortable with, then keep
on down the same path.  

I really think that we've become so megahertz, latest-gear crazy in this day
and age that we've forgotten that hardware and OS is spec'd for a job, and
if it's fulfilling that job than what's the worry?  If that wasn't the case 
you wouldn't see so many VAXes and PDPs still out there ticking.  Remember,
this isn't mickeysoft we're talking about here.  My last employer *still*
runs DEC 3000 series Alphas for some tasks.

