That's a fair argument.  My guess, following on my earlier supposition, is
that you've got 2 basic types of buyers:

1) Those that buy for OS and capability.  While I wouldn't run out and dump
bazillions into Alpha gear with the expectation of upgrading it constantly
for the next 10 years, I see no reason why sensible purchases shouldn't
continue until a definite EOL is announced for the platform.  I don't think
many places got burned on that policy with VAX, but I could be wrong

2) Those that buy purely on performance of hardware.  Most applications that
require purchase based purely on performance require constant re-evalution
of performance and a quick replacement or upgrade cycle.  Given this type of
buyer, I would imagine they'd be re-evaluating hardware in the timeframe
that Alpha will hit the transition period.  So perhaps 2002/2003 may be a
dry time period for Alpha, but I have to figure that Compaq's counted on
that.  Even still, there'll be plenty of people upgrading their current
boxen and even a few of those rare customers that buy the fastest thing
