Below is some relevant news regarding the subject:

      Zona Flash Note:

      Alpha Reaches Its Omega -
      Compaq/Intel Announce Enterprise Server Agreement

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

      Compaq Computer and Intel announced a multi-year agreement that will
result in the transfer of key Compaq enterprise server technology to Intel,
and the migration of Compaq's 64-bit server family onto Intel's Itanium
architecture. As part of the agreement, Compaq will consolidate its entire
64-bit family of servers onto the Itanium microprocessor by 2004. To support
its current enterprise customers and assist in their transition to the new
architecture, Compaq will deliver an additional generation of Alpha-based
servers through 2003, with a new system based on the Alpha EV7
microprocessor scheduled for introduction late in 2002. The company will
also aggressively advance its Proliant server roadmap based on Itanium with
the first systems due in Q3 2001. Compaq will design and build new non-stop
Himalaya systems based on MIPS chip technology until the first shipments of
Itanium-based Himalaya systems are available in 2004.

      The new generation of Itanium-based Compaq servers will support Tru64
UNIX, OpenVMS and NonStop Kernel, complementing Compaq's support of Windows
2000 and Linux, and the company will immediately begin porting its Alpha and
Himalaya based operating systems to Itanium. Compaq is also transferring
significant Alpha microprocessor and compiler technology, tools and
resources to Intel, including several hundred Alpha engineers, complier
experts and infrastructure employees who will join Intel during the next
couple of years. Finally, Compaq and Intel have agreed to joint engineering
development focused on advanced parallelism for high-end computing.

      Net/Net
      Shocking statements! Astounding agreements! Unprecedented
pronouncements! Right?

      Well, no, not really, if we paid heed to our history. On October 27,
1997, we published a Flash Note announcing an agreement between Intel and
DEC that halted a suit by DEC against Intel. The agreement included a
10-year patent cross-licensing agreement, the purchase by Intel of a DEC
chip manufacturing plant, an agreement by Intel to continue producing Alpha
chips and agreements by DEC to develop a "full line of systems" based on
Intel's IA-64 architecture in part by porting its Digital UNIX to Intel's
IA-64 "Merced" chip. At that time, we stated that the agreement signaled
"the beginning of the end of the Alpha chip as we know it" and suggested it
could signify the beginning of a migration away from non-Intel RISC-based
processing for enterprise hardware vendors. Four months later, on January
26, 1998, our Flash Note noted Compaq's acquisition of DEC, which allowed
Compaq to step directly into the corporate IT space with Alpha chip-based
products, as well as to further cement the company's already close
relationship with Intel.

      But since that time, the world of corporate hardware vendors has
undergone a number of remarkable changes. Of the Big Four (IBM, Sun, Compaq
and HP), IBM is the only player aggressively supporting a multiplicity of
hardware architectures, HP seems to continue down a path whereby PA-RISC
will fade into the sunset, with only Sun basing its business strategy on a
single, non-Intel based architecture. Intel's newest version of its IA-64
architecture, the Itanium chip, has been accepted by vendors of every
stripe, including all of the Big Four (yes, even Sun offered that Solaris
support would be forthcoming. Additionally, IBM, Compaq and HP have all made
significant commitments to support Linux across their product lines, at the
apparent expense of their proprietary UNIX operating systems. So, what
gives, exactly, and how does today's announcement fit into the larger
corporate IT landscape?

      Overall, we believe the Compaq/Intel agreement reflects a simple
economic truth about hardware; the growing disparity between increasing chip
performance and decreasing prices has led the industry to a position where
diversity is not necessarily better. Increasingly, as hardware profit
margins ease toward zero, boxes are coming to be regarded as loss leaders
that vendors can use to initiate and deliver ongoing management and
consultant services to their corporate clients. Standardizing products
across a single architecture theoretically should help vendors reduce R&D
overhead and stabilize or increase revenues, a scenario which might be
especially appealing to Compaq and HP. How HP will regard this announcement
is difficult to gauge. The Itanium is based on technology originally owned
by HP, and the company was actively involved in developing the architecture
with Intel. In fact, HP recently announced Itanium-based workstation, server
and OS products, but whether it is contemplating a complete migration to the
platform is not yet known.

      IBM and Sun are both unlikely to follow Compaq's Itanium strategy, but
for substantially different reasons. Given its sheer size, IBM qualifies as
a special case. The company's support of architectures including Intel,
AS400, and PowerPC, amongst others, is simply part and parcel of being the
vendor of choice for the world's largest enterprises. Realistically, IBM's
legacy would not permit its migration to a single architecture, though we
expect them to offer full product sets based on Itanium. Sun is also a
separate case, largely due to its long held claim that UltraSPARC + Solaris
offers users a unique advantage far beyond the capabilities of the
competition. While this argument may have been compelling in the past,
performance improvements in the ubiquitous Intel Architecture and the
increasing embrace of heterogeneous computing environments by corporate
users suggest that the ground beneath enterprise IT is shifting. Keeping Sun
on its balance in this environment will test the skills of Scott and Ed's
dynamic operation.

      After ongoing fits and starts, unmet promises, accountable and
unaccountable delays and an often unforgiving market, Intel is finally
delivering its hardware vendor clients 64-bit processing technologies that
appear to be very much ready for prime time. That's the obvious news. What
is more oblique is just what Intel will do with a chipset that allows it and
its partners to compete at the top end of whatever computing market it
chooses. In fact, Itanium could allow Intel to ease away from the desktop
product spectrum and focus its efforts on the far more lucrative server
market, though thus far, the company has made its reputation and fortunes on
being inside everything from the PC on up. We expect that Itanium's position
in the market will be a work in progress, but today's announcement
aggressively proffers that Intel is definitely no longer just about the
desktop.

      For Compaq, this announcement illustrates both remarkable
opportunities and challenges. On the opportunity side, embracing a single
architecture would allow a new platform focus that could more efficiently
(and profitably) address their customer's needs. The move could also produce
savings in product support and perhaps improve customer satisfaction. But
customer satisfaction will also be a key challenge in the company's journey
to an Itanium future. How effective Compaq will be at convincing, satisfying
and migrating existing Alpha and MIPS customers to the new architecture
remains to be seen. Additionally, though Compaq has pledged its support
Tru64 UNIX, OpenVMS and NonStop Kernel through this transition, how VARs and
consultants will approach the new Itanium products (and how long and well
they will continue to support legacy DEC and Tandem applications) is
uncertain. In essence, the future that Compaq and Intel envision represents
a Greenfield restart that will perhaps rate as significant as Microsoft's
(and the PC industry's) shift from DOS with the introduction of Windows 95.
However, the costs associated with upgrading or replacing a single desktop
operating system are chump change compared to migrating an enterprise to an
entirely new server architecture. While we believe that Compaq has made all
the right promises and is preparing itself for the challenge to come, the
experience is not likely to be painless.

      So what winners and losers will arise from this announcement? On the
far side of the wire, we see IBM, HP and Sun trailing this particular field.
Sun, especially, seems out of the running, given the company's dedication to
a chip architecture of which it is effectively the sole vendor. This makes
Sun either the smartest or slowest kid on the block, but only time and
enterprise customers hold the solution to this particular riddle. Though
IBM's products are so widely accepted and dispersed as to make it immune to
many portions of the Compaq/Intel agreement, we do wonder how Compaq's shift
to Itanium for its Himalaya line might affect Big Blue, whose main frames
and high-end servers could eventually feel some pressure from the deal. HP
is the toughest to handicap. Given the company's pivotal role in developing
Itanium, it would have made more sense to see HP sign an agreement such as
this one, but with their ongoing managerial and financial issues it's not
surprising to hear their silence. Additionally, Compaq's significant
transfer of Alpha technology and personnel to Intel could put HP and its own
Itanium products at a competitive disadvantage further down the road.

      From where we sit, Compaq and Intel are crossing this particular
finish line nose and nose, after helping one another along the way. The
agreement effectively converts two of Intel's high-end chip competing chip
architectures, Alpha and Himalaya, to Itanium, leaving PowerPC, UltraSPARC,
and an ever decreasing number of alternatives to resist what appears to be
an ongoing migration of enterprise computing to the Intel platform. Compaq
gains leverage across its entire enterprise product line, and the potential
for major cost savings that the company would enjoy for years to come. In
essence, today's agreement should relieve Compaq of most of the costs and
headaches of microprocessor R&D, and allow the company to better focus its
attentions on building and satisfying its enterprise customer base. In
today's tight economy, that qualifies as a major benefit. Overall, by
ushering Alpha to its natural omega, Compaq has positioned itself
aggressively, imaginatively and strategically for many, many races to come.

