jra@dorothy.msas.net (Jay R. Ashworth) wrote:
> *Right* in the middle of the appendectomy, Dan Riley turned
>    to Hawkeye and me and said:
> > Sergei Morozov <smv@stanford.edu> writes:
> > > Furthermore, it'll take years to port Tru64
> > 
> > Why?  Unlike Linux, OSF/1 was designed to be portable.
> 
> For sufficiently small values of "unlike".
> 
> How many platforms is OSF/1 on?

"was designed to be portable" and "has been ported" are
not the same thing.  OSF was a collaborative project,
involving DEC, HP, IBM, and others as a reaction to a
looming AT&T/Sun joint Unix project.  OSF/1, DEC's
implementation, was the only one to actually ship, but
it was desgined with portability in mind.

Linux, on the other hand, was desgined *specifically*
for the 80386 from the beginning; if you don't believe
me, then get it straight from the horse's mouth:

http://www.dina.dk/~abraham/Linus_vs_Tanenbaum.html#linus1

Of course, those early design features have been 
"corrected" by now, but Mr. Riley is correct.

