Hallo!

 EP> Seems that all of their strategic announcements for the 
 EP> Alpha line has never been completed as promised.  What makes 
 EP> anyone 
 EP> believe this is this
 EP> different?

I think the main problem for the whole story is not what Compaq or 
Intel will do, but how the customers do react to announcements like 
this one.

I know that there are few customers with big contracts that care a shit 
about rumours and just buy what works for them *now*. However there are 
also many more that don't see it this way.

We have already seen common reactions in this group. People say:

- "Why buy an alpha system now? The alpha is dead, and we don't want to 
end
  up with a CPU that will soon be unsupported. Ok, if they have tru64 
on 
  ia64, we may buy tru64 again - but until then, we better don't buy 
Compaq."

- "With a new processor, binary compatibility is broken anyway. So now 
this
  is a good time to switch to something entirely different, if we can't
  maintain the current line anyway."

- "First DEC has been sold to Compaq, then Compaq sells the Alpha. What 
will
  be sold or discontinued next? VMS? Tru64? Everything?"

- "If we need to buy IA64, we can just as well use Linux on it".

- "We bought Compaq because the Alpha is a great processor. Great 
vectorizing
  FPU, 64-bit since 19whatever, rock proven, and non-Intel. But if 
Compaq
  is just IA64, we could by *that* from about anywhere".

Independent about if these quotings are true or not, and independent 
about what Compaq will actually do or not, the problem is the 
atmosphere among the user base. Once users start grumbling too much 
about the vendor, the system will die. Not for rational arguments, but 
for pure psychology. We've seen this many times. 

Personally, I have been likeing DEC for delivering solid solutions with 
everything from the same vendor: The CPU, the OS, the Workstation. 
Solid work, designed to work together flawlessly, and if anything ever 
goes wrong there is one single vendor I need to contact for support, 
and there are good logging tools in the OS that even allow me to solve 
most problems on my own which otherwise would be undiagnosable because 
on other systems, the OS and the Hardware don't communicate as well. 
Also, even though I am by far not that long into DEC stuff like some 
other persons in here, "DEC"-type solutions have become kind of a place 
where I feel at home for me. I trust this brand to deliver solutions 
for my problems, and I trust that while there may be some obstacles 
sometimes, there always will be a way to get around them. Now the 
problem is that when Compaq starts to tear this "DEC" brand apart in 
pieces, it is just like in all other events when something that I had 
known and become fond of is suddenly disrupted. If I had invested a 
decade or more of building DEC knowledge like some others, I would 
surely be just as upset as they are.

While it is true that Compaq of course can also ship good solutions 
with a foreign processor (in fact I as a user don't really see anything 
about the processor as long as it works, you could probably sell me an 
IA64 machine without me noticing it unless I'd try to run Alpha 
binaries on it or start viewing disassemblies), it is simply the 
psychology that makes me fear that things are likely to worsen in the 
future. And I am sure that while I would not base a decision what to 
buy on such psyhcology, I am not in the place of making such decisions, 
and I know that others, in particularly also the persons responsible 
for these things at places where I work, *do* care about this sort of 
psychology. And this only intensifies my fear that things get worse, 
because if fewer poeple buy Compaq, Compaq will reduce its expenses for 
new developmends, and this is a dead-end circle.

Of course I understand economic realities, and maybe selling the Alpha 
and converting to IA64 really was the only viable choice. But the way 
how Compaq communicated this to is customers was clearly suboptimal, 
and *this* may hit back on Compaq more then the decision to sell the 
Alpha itself. It all too much looks like some manager had simply looked 
at some charts and decided to sell the stuff without asking the 
engineers and developers, and most important without thinking about how 
customers would view this decision.

Let's hope that this is not how it happened in reality.

Viele Gr��e,
Tobias



