But look at that competition - I'm not so sure it's a stiff as people
think...

AIX 5L - from what I've seen so far, it's AIX meets Linux, in which case I
imagine most would choose to do without the AIX...

HP-UX  - as a current HP-UX admin, I have doubts about HP-UX's ability to
survive the transition.  HP's clearly a very divided company, and HP-UX is
not yet available on IA-64 (whilst HP has spent *much* time on Linux for
IA-64).  Furthermore, HP-UX has been slipping behind most of the Unix
competition the last few years in functionality and technology.  11i helped
some, but it's still got a lot to do to catch up.

Linux - okay, here's where the real competition is, Windows aside.  The
question is do you really believe that Linux will catch up enough in a few
years time to make Tru64 and it's proven capabilities irrelevant?  Tru64 has
a lot to brag about (although Compaq's done a poor job of bragging), and
with the Oracle 9i announcements it's clear there's some strong ISV support
in sectors still.

Solaris still could be released on IA-64.  In fact, with this announcment on
Compaq's part, I'd almost guarantee that they'll have to release just to
pony up.  That's another source of competition, although Sun has always been
pretty bassackwards on their Solaris x86 product in the past and I have no
reason to believe that this will change in the future, as Solaris IA-64 will
not sell UltraSPARC servers.

I would say that Compaq's got as good a chance as anybody else at being the
dominant IA-64 Unix.  Furthermore, the original "Bravo" IA-64 port of Tru64
(the one everyone seems to have forgotten) was only "abandonded" because of
Compaq's commitments to support SCO, who isn't really a going concern for
anybody anymore...Given the fact that "Bravo" wasn't actually ever abandoned
due to contractual obligations (see other posts for more details), the port
will be *very* trivial to complete, I would imagine.

While I'm confused, betrayed, and angry over the death knell for Alpha, I
have to admit that Tru64 on IA-64 makes me overall more positive about
Tru64's mainstream future.  Tru64 had been become increasingly more
marginalized and shoved into tight niches - this *might* be the move that
brings it forefront.  Essentially, I'll be watching, Compaq - don't screw
this one up or you'll never be forgiven at all for the way you've blundered
about with Alpha...

