Hallo LoneStar!

 L> are there any incompatibilities or hits that I need to be 
 L> aware of in order to use both Compaq C and cygnus gcc on a 5.1 
 L> system?
 L> Do I need to change anything to use gcc in a way that could 
 L> alter regular Compaq C functionality?

Except for the problem that 5.0 is currently the last Tru64 variant 
where gcc functions properly (...), both are perfectly interoperable. 
You can just install gcc and then use "cc" and "cxx" to run Compaq C, 
and "gcc" and "g++" to run gcc. Install gcc in a separate prefix, like 
/usr/local/gcc-2.9.3, and then just make symlinks 
/usr/local/bin/gcc -> /usr/local/gcc-2.9.3/bin/gcc
and likewhise for g++, and you'll be done and sure that gcc did not 
have a chance to overwrite anything of Compaq C.

As for the 5.1 problem, you'd best search the Tru64 Managers list (go 
to www.tru64.org, then use the search field on the top) for this topic. 
It seems that gcc 3 can be made to run on 5.1 when you use a lot of 
patches ...

The problem lies buried in the fact that 5.1 has a different assembler 
than 5.0(a), and gcc cannot use the GNU assembler on Tru64, but has to 
use DEC's assembler.

We stick with 5.0 until this problem is solved, therefore :-).

Viele Gr��e,
