In article <9bbdf680.0106050123.7be20ef4@posting.google.com>, andysands@yahoo.com (Andy Sands) writes:
> 1) If a system IS NOT configured to use enhanced security, so that the
> encrypted user passwords are still stored in the world readable
> /etc/passwd file, does the existence of the asterisk (*) in the
> password field indicate that the account cannot be used to login to
> the system?
> 
> In these circumstances are 'nologin' '*nologin' and '*' all valid
> login disablers for the password field?

One generally assumes any legible field would not be the result of a
crypt() hash, so the account is effectively disabled.  In particular
short fields don't have storage enough for the seed.  It might be
possible to pick a password which does crypt() to a legible string, but
at least nobody is likely to know what that password is by reading the
string.

> 2) If a system IS configured to use enhanced security, what value
> should be placed in the password field in /etc/passwd to disable
> logins to that profile?

See other posts.

> 3) If no login_shell is associated with a userid in /etc/passwd, does
> this also disable logins to that profile, or does the system assign a
> default login_shell from somewhere?

Generally a login shell of /bin/true allows the account to "log in", but
doesn't actually prompt for anything before logging out.  Sometimes this
is usefull for setting up an account to be used for non-interactive
purposes (for example, it might be acceptable to an FTP deamon, don't
know if it is on Tru64).

