In article <thp94aoko6b81f@xo.supernews.co.uk>, "AW" <awtelem@globalnet.co.uk> writes:

>I've got a DEC Alpha 4100 and Digital UNIX V4.0F (Rev. 1229). This machine
>has now (for the first time) got a tape device, a TZ877 DLT tape unit. I can
>see the device (sh dev) from the >>> prompt, but when I build the
>kernel.....no sign of it!

If a new SCSI device is on an existing SCSI bus, it isn't necessary to
rebuild the kernel to look for that device.  If the device is on a new
bus, you have to rebuild the kernel so that it will use the new adapter
and bus.  For that you either have to update the configuration file by
hand or boot genvmunix and let it write a new configuration file for 
you.  The procedure is probably in the system management documentation.

Your statement of the program doesn't include enough information about
the configuration to make better guesses at what the problem might be.
SCSI problems are often connection related so a more detailed hardware
description would be useful.

To take full advantage of the changer, you need something that licenses
the CLC Media Changer driver (on the Associated Product CDROM).  Many
versions of the TZ877 shipped with a copy of MRU, which can be used
to control the changer and does license the driver.  For V4.0F don't 
use the media changer driver included on the MRU distribution, use the
one on the Associated Products CDROM.

># cd /dev
>
># ls -l *rmt*
>
>*rmt* not found
>

I'm pretty sure that V4 will create missing special device for tapes
when it boot, if it can see the tape drive.  Check the boot messages
to see if kernel saw it, and then work from there.
