In article <991734380@f1.n7600.z242.fido.de>,
Tobias Ernst <tobi@bland.fido.de> wrote:
> 
> The problem lies buried in the fact that 5.1 has a different assembler 
> than 5.0(a), and gcc cannot use the GNU assembler on Tru64, but has to 
> use DEC's assembler.

When I was reading the release notes for 5.1, when I got to the note
about the new assembler, my first thought was "Hmmm, I bet this will break
gcc."  Imagine my surprise when I was right.  ;-)

Luckily, the new assembler provides a command line flag (mentioned in
the release notes, I can't find it mentioned in the man page) that will
invoke the older assembler.

This means that gcc can be built and used to just invoke the older assembler,
until the new one works with gcc (may even work now, if patch kit #3 is
installed -- I'm not sure).  There are at least two ways you might get
this to work:

1) create a wrapper script named `as', make it executable, and just have
it exec the system `as' with the additional option to get the older assembler.
Make sure this is in gcc's build directory while building, and in the platform
tools directory after installation.

2) once gcc is installed, you might be able to get rid of the wrapper script
altogether.  gcc uses (?) a file called `specs' that gets installed deep
under the lib/gcc-lib directory that has entries for how to invoke various
tools.  There *might* be an entry for `as'.  If you add the necessary flag
to get the old as, that *might* be all gcc needs, from that point on.

I haven't tried either of these on 5.1, but #1 should be fairly easy
to accomplish.

Tim
