Jerome Bellet wrote:
> 
> Sorry if this question has already been asked.
> Apparently Tru64 4.0 (& 5.0) does not support NIS+ natively.
> Is there any port of NIS+ for this OS ?
> 
> Alternatively, if I have to keep NIS on my alpha boxes, is it 
> advisable to install NIS+ on the other hosts with NIS compatability 
> on, or should I also configure NIS on them?


To NIS+ or not to NIS+, that is the question !  Boy do I have the scars
to prove it !!

First, NIS+ **IS** being phased out !!  It is not even on the primary
distribution for Solaris 8.  No major Un*x vendors bought into NIS+
because of licensing fees ($$$).  Sun's replacement for NIS+ is
something called the "Federated Naming Service".

The only reason we went to NIS+ in our heterogeneous environment (Tru64
V4.0F and Solaris 2.6) is because our Corporate standards require
password and account expirations that Sun can not do with NIS (but Tru64
does quite nicely when you turn on Enhanced Security).  We ran into many
bugs (and many patches) and a very limited knowledge of NIS+ even inside
Sun.

At the moment, our users are required to have 2 passwords; one in Tru64
and one in Solaris.  Because we are using different encryption methods
(bigcrypt on Tru64) there is no way we can "synchronize" these password
for the users (although, with lots of effort, we do synchronize password
and account expirations). 

Our current plan is to use the Solaris "Plugable Authentication Module"
(pam) to validate the username and password on the Tru64 NIS master.

My advice, rethink why you are using NIS+ because it is going away.


Jack Patteeuw


