As others have written NIS+ is not going to be supported - the biggest reason
was the money. There is though a plan in the works to support at least the name
server switch piece.  You may want to check out the tru64.org site - there's a
phorum section there about "new features" where NIS+ was queried about
recently...

with respect to the LDAP queries...
Check out the Tru64 OSIS Solutions
(http://www.tru64unix.compaq.com/internet/osis.htm) - (it's being renamed to
"Internet Express").  Included within OSIS is an LDAP SIA mechanism that will
allow you to synchronize your passwords.

with respect to Kerberos/LDAP...
In the next release, as part of the Base OS (5.1A) we will begin to support
Kerberos credentials.  The "phased in" approach we took handles the local
authentication cases only.  In a release following that the network apps will be
kerberized. Once released search for the SSO (Single Sign On) docs/information.
The code is also integrated with the LDAP Caching Daemon which can get UNIX
style cred info (uid, gid, gecos, shell, directory) from a W2K Active
Directory...

As for the PAM that was mentioned...
Having a PAM plug-in on Tru64 would be a trick...

It is possible however to configure Tru64 Enhanced Security to not use bigcrypt.
If you wanted to go with a weaker password - you could configure your Tru64
Enhanced Security system to utilize "crypt" passwords - thus essentially
allowing for the same password as your Solaris system, although I wouldn't
recommend it.  Of course this still doesn't provide an easy way to keep your
passwords synchronized unless you wrote an SIA mechanism (similar to writing a
PAM plug-in) that handled doing the passcode updates.

--

John Ferlan
Tru64 UNIX Security
Compaq Computer Corporation




