I'm looking at the error:

    42  lseek() failed with operating system error 22 (Invalid argument)

AdvFS will _ONLY_ return this error (EINVAL) for 2 reasons.  The first
is if the type of lseek operation is neither SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, nor
SEEK_END.  These are typically hard coded in most lseek calls, so I
rather doubt that it was from this.

The other reason for returning EINVAL from AdvFS lseek() is if the new
offset results in a location before the beginning of the file.  This
can happen several ways depending on whether SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR, or
SEEK_END was specified.  For example, is SEEK_SET and a negative offset
is specified.  If SEEK_CUR and a negative offset and the current offset
plus the negative offset results in a negative value (1000 + (-2000)). 
Or finally, SEEK_END and a negative offset and the size of the file
plus the negative offset result in a negative value (65000 + (-85963)).

Since you say there are gigabyte sized files involved, then it is my
guess that this was a 32 bit signed integer overflow.  For example,
1.6GB is about 1,717,986,918 and if 429,496,730 is added to it, the
resulting value is 2,147,483,648 or in hex 0x80000000 which as a signed
integer would actually be a negative number because the most
significent sign bit is set.  so any internal math using 32 bit signed
integers could have caused an integer overflow and resulted in a
negative offset to be passed to lseek(fd,offset,SEEK_SET).

That is my guess, but I do not own the db sources, nor the methods you
were using to access the database.

And I could be way off base and just guessing wrong.

