> Context switching is generally higher on UNIX than on VMS.  Fact of
> life.  Sorry, I don't know Tru64 internals well enough to really 
> answer your questions, but I think you're on the right track.

Yep, Tru64 ain't VMS.  Very few "knobs" to twiddle  (I go back to the 
days of SRPs, IRPs and LRPs).  vmstat is a poor tool to try to monitor 
things with.  As long as you aren't swaping and you haven't hit any of 
the ulimits, sit back, relax and watch the blinking lights.

One thing that drove me crazy for a long time was, there never was much 
free memory.  I kept worrying about it until I realized that Tru64 uses 
all free memory to make more buffers !!!  If you have a graphical 
monitoring tool you will se that a small amount of swap will suddenly 
cause Tru64 to "dump" a bunch of buffers amking a large amount of free 
memory and there by stopping the swapping.  If this doesn't happen you 
need more physical memory.

Jack Patteeuw


