I'd just boot the generic kernel directly:

>>>boot -fl s -fi "genvmunix"

If it sees the new adapter and doesn't crash anything, then it
was probably just a problem with the other kernel not liking
the new adapter.  From here you can build a new kernel.  I
haven't done this on V5, but on V4 I would just:

Mount the local file systems.

# bcheckrc

Build a kernel:

# doconfig

Copy the existing kernel to a safe place.

# cp /vmunix /vmunix.sav

Copy the new kernel to /vmunix.

# cp /sys/WHATEVER/vmunix /

Reboot, to single user, to verify nothing unexpected happens and
then continue to multi-user.  See if the System Management guide
has other recommendations.  The advantage of booting /genvmunix
directly, is that you can use it a quick check that it can see
the KZPCA and doesn't crash.

