>Thank you for your thoughts.  Yes, I meant to say "double what my root
>partition is currently using" and not double the size of vmunix (posting before
>my daily coffee dose is obviously contraindicated!!!).  I was indeed thinking
>of short stroking at hardware level but was wondering how much of a benefit it
>offered when using AdvFS.  From what you've said, it appears that it doesn't do
>AdvFS much good and might even be detrimental.  Hmmmm.

Since I mentioned, short-stroking (it is hard to tell without a minimal
attribution), some extra comment might be useful.  If you don't use the
rest of the disk for anything else, the reduction in seek distance may
help performance, especially as the domain gets full.  I did some quick
I/O traces of loads on an AdvFS domain that became progressively full.
At each iteration of filling the disk, the average seek distance for
the load went up, presumably to update the log which was back towards
the beginning of the partition.

Now, you have the odd problem.  If you use the whole disk, you get more
space to work with, but longer seek distances as the disk fills.  Maintaining
locality of reference does not seem to be something AdvFS tries to do.
By reducing the available space, you can limit the distance, but to see
the benefit, the domain still has to be pretty full.  If your load tends
to fill the disk, then delete a bunch of stuff, your load will probably
be scattered across the whole disk and the seek optimization from short-
stroking may help.  But, making a defragmentation pass may pull all the
allocated data closer to the beginning, which will much the same affect
as limiting the capacity.

It is an interesting question...
