>but... wouldn't be nice to have the question answered by
>some of the experienced Tru64 systems administrators out there?
>I want to know if this is possible:
>#cat fstab
>/dev/rz0a        /      ufs rw 1 1
>#/dev/rz0b        swap1  ufs sw 0 2
>#primary swap, b partition,other disk
>/dev/rz1b       swap1  ufs sw 0 2


In past versions (maybe only on ULTRIX), the primary dump space had to
be on the same disk as the root in order to use the console device
drivers to dump memory to disk in the event of a crash.  Early versions
of DEC OSF/1 may have had this restriction or at least preference,
but I suspect the "usually" is an indication that it isn't required.

You can have more than one page/swap space, so unless you have
other plans for there's no particular reason not to use the B
partition on rz0.

Also, check the manual page for fstab to see if a leading # on a
line is a legal comment character.  While it is for many similar
files, it may not be for this one.  I know this was a problem
on ULTRIX.  Habit has kept me from finding out on Tru64 UNIX.
If you want to keep a line in, but don't want it to be used,
use "xx" instead "rw", "sw", "ro" or "rq" in the appropriate
field.
