Tobias Ernst wrote:
.
.
.
> Adjusting things on the fly can be done without a AdvFS Utilities
> license. You basically only need the AdvFS Utilities license to have a
> cool graphical UI, and for some more advanced features like combining
> more than one disk into a single AdvFS volume.

I **LOVE** AdvFS !!!  I don't know how a sys admins can live without it
when dealing with large disks or RAID sets (can you say 88 Gb RAID sets
?)  On non-root/non-bootable drives I just use the C: partition (100% of
the disk) as one AdvFS File Domain and the "slice it up" as need be with
as many File Sets as I need. 

On root/bootable devices (or a device that will have some swap on it)
one of these drives, I determine how much swap I want in B: and put the
reset in A: making A: my only File Domain.

Stop thinking about "hard portioning" (except for swap).  Make one File
Domain per device and think of File Sets as a relatively unlimited
number of soft partition that you can add or delete on the fly !!!!

Jack Patteeuw


